TY  - JOUR
TI  - Arts-based psycho-social programs for Muslim refugees and refugees from Muslim-majority countries.
AU  - Powell, Sara
T2  - Dissertation Abstracts International Section A: Humanities and Social Sciences
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
AB  - This qualitative case study explored the psycho-social needs of Muslim refugees and refugees from Muslim majority countries and examined the contributions of arts-based interventions in addressing these needs. Framed within cross-cultural psychology and social justice theory, the study involved eight stakeholders including volunteers, art therapists, psychologists, artists, educators, and conflict resolution specialists from organizations operating in Jordan, Turkiye, the United Kingdom, France, and the United Arab Emirates. Data were thematically analyzed using NVivo software, producing five core themes: Basic Survival Needs and Psychosocial Support; Trauma Recovery; Cultural Sensitivity and Empowerment through Art; Logistical and Cultural Challenges; and Assimilation and Social Integration through Art. Findings revealed that arts-based psycho-social programs provided non-verbal and culturally grounded avenues for trauma processing, emotional expression, adjustment, social connection, and the preservation of cultural and religious identity. Such programs functioned as relational and symbolic spaces for identity reconstruction, particularly when embedded within cultural and faith-sensitive frameworks. Refugees were empowered to engage with host communities while maintaining religious and cultural values, facilitating levels of social integration without assimilation. Legal precarity, gender norms, and infrastructural barriers hindered access and participation. This study contributes to international psychology by demonstrating the global applicability of culturally responsive and ethically attuned psycho-social models. It further advances trauma psychology by validating non-pathologizing, community-based, and arts integrated responses to displacement-related trauma. The findings underscore the imperative for scalable, rights based, and sustainable arts-based interventions, particularly for Muslim refugees navigating intersecting layers of exclusion. Addressing unmet survival needs emerged as a critical prerequisite to psycho-social engagement, reaffirming that trauma recovery, acculturation, and social cohesion are inseparable from broader structural conditions. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
