T3  - Transcultural Psychiatric Research Review
AB  - Initiated a special project to investigate the mental health status of East Timorese asylum seekers, using a 'Researcher-Advocacy' model that combined elements of advocacy, quantitative and qualitative research, and strategic assistance in a program of engagement with this marginalized group. 33 consecutive asylum-seeker clients (aged 23-67 yrs) attending a newly formed clinic participated in the study, representing a quarter of the known population of asylum seekers from East Timor living in Sydney at the time. High levels of trauma, including torture and other human rights abuses, were recorded. Respondents also reported a wide range of resettlement and adaptational difficulties, particularly relating to their uncertain residency status. 80% met criteria for one or more psychiatric disorder. The wider benefits of the study included the extension of services to a group that previously had shown a reluctance to seek assistance for traumatic stress, the engagement of the exile community as a whole, and building the capacity to respond both in Australia and in East Timor to the humanitarian emergency of 1999. Scientific limitations of the model included the labor-intensive nature of the program, the small and selective sample recruited and incomplete data collection. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
