TI  - Vicarious Burnout and Opportunities to Thrive: Support Personnel Exposed to Female-Specific Refugee Trauma.
AU  - Taheri, Melika
AU  - Fitzpatrick, Sally
AU  - McCormack, Lynne
T2  - Canadian Psychology / Psychologie Canadienne
AB  - Despite an ever-increasing rise in displaced populations, there is a paucity of research exploring the perspective of support personnel exposed to the narratives of refugee trauma specific to women. This idiographic study explored the professional lived experiences of eight support personnel exposed to women's refugee trauma narratives postsettlement in Australia. Semistructured interviews were conducted and analyzed according to the protocols of Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis. Three group experiential themes, gender and cultural minimization, conflictual views, and growth in action, overarched personal experiential themes and reflected a journey of new knowledge, cognitive challenge, and vicarious exposure in support personnel from exposure to gender-specific trauma narratives of women from refugee backgrounds, now settled in Australia. Stories of gender and cultural minimization before fleeing their homelands and throughout the women' s refugee journey challenged the participants to examine basic human rights concepts and refine for themselves the definition of a refugee. Conflictual views toward the women they supported, combined with a decentralized, complex support system, further imposed an extensive strain on the participants. Their own growth in action came from attempts to promote authentic support structures that could benefit support personnel, women from refugee backgrounds, and host societies. Consequently, within this study, despite the risk of vicarious trauma and burnout for these participants, intersectional integration and systemic support inadvertently ignited opportunities for vicarious growth in these participants. These findings incorporated United Nations sustainability goals of good health and well-being, gender equality, and reduced inequalities from a non-Western collectivist viewpoint.
CY  - Ottawa, Ontario
