TI  - O73 - When I can be my whole authentic self, I feel safe and know that I belong: A photovoice study exploring equity-oriented pregnancy care from the perspectives of Karen women of refugee background in Victoria, Australia...Australian College of Midwives National Conference – Be the Change, September 12-14, 2023, Adelaide, South Australia
AU  - Coe, Ms Rowena
AU  - Toke, Ms Shadow
AU  - Wallace, Heather
AU  - Riggs, Elisha
AU  - Brown, Stephanie J
AU  - Szwarc, Mr Jo
AU  - Biggs, Laura
T2  - Women & Birth
AB  - Inequitable pregnancy care experiences and outcomes disproportionately affect women and babies of refugee backgrounds. Equitable pregnancy care is possible when care is culturally safe, however only women are able to define what culturally safe care is to them. It is therefore critical that women's voices inform our understanding of cultural safety within pregnancy care. This study sought to understand what culturally safe pregnancy care is to Karen women of refugee background residing in Victoria, Australia. A photovoice design informed by participatory-based research principles was used. A Karen Community Researcher established a Community Advisory Group comprised of recent mothers and community leaders to inform study design and conduct. Karen women with experience of pregnancy care in Victoria were invited to take photos which reflected their perspectives of cultural safety within pregnancy care and to attend a series of discussion groups. Discussion groups were co-facilitated by the Community Researcher and lead researcher in English and S'gaw Karen. Audio recordings were translated and transcribed by discussion group facilitators, with transcripts undergoing reflexive thematic analysis. Five women aged 34 to 40 years old participated in the study. All women had received pregnancy care in Victoria in the previous 1 to 9 years. Analysis identified four themes: (1) sharing power through storytelling; (2) building foundations for belonging; (3) I'll do it myself; and (4) cultivating reciprocal curiosity. These themes sit within the overarching theme: When I can be my whole authentic self, I feel safe and know that I belong. Women want opportunities to get to know the people caring for them by sharing stories. Reciprocal curiosity between women, midwives and obstetricians makes culturally safe pregnancy care possible. To our knowledge, this is the first Australian study to ask women of refugee background what culturally safe pregnancy care is to them. Findings demonstrate that cultural safety relies on Karen women being free to embrace their cultural and spiritual identity without fear of racism and discrimination during pregnancy care. This study offers important insights to inform midwifery practice and education. By listening to women, and sharing a little of themselves during care, midwives and obstetricians can strengthen refugee background women's pathways to equitable pregnancy care.
