TI  - The effect of societal trauma and institutional betrayal on mass psychology and political distrust
AU  - Aydın, Bengisu Nehir
T2  - Political psychology
A2  - Östürk, Erdinç
T3  - Türkiye Klinikleri Psychology — Special Topics
AB  - ABS TRACT Societal traumas are devastating events that cause physical and psychological harm to a group, disrupting their sense of identity, beliefs, and connection. These traumas often leave long-lasting psychological effects on both individuals and communities, which can be transmitted across generations. As a collective entity, society often struggles to process such widespread pain and loss. This chapter explores the effects of societal trauma through the lens of institutional betrayal, which profoundly undermines both individual and mass trust, and examines its connection to political distrust from a dissoanalytic perspective. Dissoanalysis theory argues that societal dissociation, focused on the experience of the whole, almost abrades the societal bonds. Communities within the same society experience unique dis-sociations, transforming from psychosocial dissociation to mass dissociation. Political distrust and dis-sociation are issues that stem from a combination of demobilizing emotions, such as cynicism, despair, shame, and a feeling of powerlessness, which discourage political action. Political distrust rooted in societal trauma and institutional betrayal trauma is complex, but understanding these complex dynamics is essential to addressing and mending the degraded relationship between citizens and institutions. Addressing the sources of betrayal is often necessary to begin restoring trust and healing long-standing societal wounds.
